{
  "body": [
    {
      "inlines": [
        {
          "inlines": [
            {
              "text": "Previous posts",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "internal": false,
          "target": "https://blog.rust-lang.org/2015/04/10/Fearless-Concurrency.html",
          "type": "link"
        },
        {
          "text": " have covered two pillars of Rust's design:",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "text": "Memory safety without garbage collection",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "text": "Concurrency without data races",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          }
        ]
      ],
      "ordered": false,
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "inlines": [
        {
          "text": "This post begins exploring the third pillar:",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": "Abstraction without overhead",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          }
        ]
      ],
      "ordered": false,
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "inlines": [
        {
          "text": "One of the mantras of C++, one of the qualities that make it a good fit for systems programming, is its principle of zero-cost abstraction:",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "inlines": [
            {
              "text": "C++ implementations obey the zero-overhead principle: What you don't use, you don't pay for [Stroustrup, 1994]. And further: What you do use, you couldn't hand code any better.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "inlines": [
            {
              "text": "-- Stroustrup",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "paragraph"
        }
      ],
      "type": "quote"
    },
    {
      "inlines": [
        {
          "text": "This mantra did not always apply to Rust, which for example used to have mandatory garbage collection. But over time Rust's ambitions have gotten ever lower-level, and zero-cost abstraction is now a core principle.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "inlines": [
        {
          "text": "The cornerstone of abstraction in Rust is ",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "emphasis": true,
          "text": "traits",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": ":",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": "Traits are Rust's sole notion of interface",
                "type": "text"
              },
              {
                "text": ". A trait can be implemented by multiple types, and in fact new traits can provide implementations for existing types. On the flip side, when you want to abstract over an unknown type, traits are how you specify the few concrete things you need to know about that type.",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": "Traits can be statically dispatched",
                "type": "text"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Like C++ templates, you can have the compiler generate a separate copy of an abstraction for each way it is instantiated. This comes back to the C++ mantra of \"What you do use, you couldn't hand code any better\" -- the abstraction ends up completely erased.",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": "Traits can be dynamically dispatched",
                "type": "text"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Sometimes you really do need an indirection, and so it doesn't make sense to \"erase\" an abstraction at runtime. The ",
                "type": "text"
              },
              {
                "emphasis": true,
                "text": "same",
                "type": "text"
              },
              {
                "text": " notion of interface -- the trait -- can also be used when you want to dispatch at runtime.",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": "Traits solve a variety of additional problems beyond simple abstraction",
                "type": "text"
              },
              {
                "text": ". They are used as \"markers\" for types, like the ",
                "type": "text"
              },
              {
                "text": "Send",
                "type": "code"
              },
              {
                "text": " marker described ",
                "type": "text"
              },
              {
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "text": "in a previous post",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "internal": false,
                "target": "https://blog.rust-lang.org/2015/04/10/Fearless-Concurrency.html",
                "type": "link"
              },
              {
                "text": ". They can be used to define \"extension methods\" -- that is, to add methods to an externally-defined type. They largely obviate the need for traditional method overloading. And they provide a simple scheme for operator overloading.",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          }
        ]
      ],
      "ordered": false,
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "inlines": [
        {
          "text": "All told, the trait system is the secret sauce that gives Rust the ergonomic, expressive feel of high-level languages while retaining low-level control over code execution and data representation.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "inlines": [
        {
          "text": "This post will walk through each of the above points at a high level, to give you a sense for how the design achieves these goals, without getting too bogged down in the details.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "h:background-methods-in-rust",
      "inlines": [
        {
          "text": "Background: methods in Rust",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "level": 3,
      "type": "heading"
    },
    "<54 more items>"
  ],
